course: geography
lesson: 42
title: Plate tectonics
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Earth's hard outer shell, the lithosphere, is cracked into giant pieces called plates, which fit together like a jigsaw that has been dropped on the floor.
explain: The plates are always moving, very slowly, about as fast as your fingernails grow, carried on the slowly flowing mantle below.
explain: The strongest push comes from the edges: old, cold ocean plate is heavy, sinks into the mantle and drags the rest of the plate behind it, like a tablecloth sliding off a table once enough of it hangs over the edge.
explain: What happens at a plate's edge depends on how the plates meet: pulling apart makes new sea floor, one diving under another makes trenches and volcanoes, two continents crashing makes mountains, and plates grinding sideways make earthquakes.
explain: For fifty years after Alfred Wegener said the continents move, most scientists laughed; then striped magnetic rocks on the sea floor proved him right, and today GPS measures the movement every day.
fact: The lithosphere is broken into about 15 large plates and many smaller ones that fit together like a cracked jigsaw.
fact: Plates move about 2-10 cm a year, roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.
fact: The biggest force moving plates is slab pull: old, cold ocean plate is heavy and sinks into the mantle, dragging the rest behind it.
fact: Where plates move apart, magma rises to fill the gap and makes new crust, as along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge.
fact: Where an ocean plate meets another plate, the denser one dives down in a subduction zone, making a deep trench and a line of volcanoes.
fact: When two continents collide, neither sinks, because continental crust is too light, so the crust crumples upwards into huge mountains, such as the Himalayas, where India is still pushing into Asia.
fact: Where plates slide past each other sideways, crust is neither made nor destroyed, but strong earthquakes happen, as along the San Andreas Fault in California.
fact: About three-quarters of the world's active volcanoes and most big earthquakes lie around the Pacific, in the Ring of Fire.
fact: Alfred Wegener proposed in 1912 that continents drift, but most scientists rejected the idea until the 1960s, partly because he could not explain what moved them.
fact: The sea floor has stripes of rock magnetised in alternate directions, matching on both sides of ocean ridges, because the magnetic field flips now and then while new crust forms at the ridge.
fact: Today satellites measure plate movement directly: GPS stations show Europe and North America moving apart about 2.5 cm a year.
exercise: What is the Earth's lithosphere broken into? | About 15 large plates and many smaller ones | One single unbroken shell of hard rock all round | Thousands of plates, all of exactly the same size | Exactly seven plates, one for each continent
exercise: How fast do plates usually move? | About 2-10 cm a year, like fingernails grow | About 2-10 km a year, like a very slow walk | About 1 metre a day | They do not move at all
exercise: What is slab pull? | Old, cold, heavy ocean plate sinking and dragging the plate behind it | The Moon pulling the plates across the Earth as it goes round each month | Wind dragging the continents along | Rivers pushing plates apart with their water
exercise: What happens where two plates move apart? | Magma rises and makes new crust | The ground sinks into a deep trench | Two continents crumple into mountains | Crust is destroyed and melts away
exercise: What is the Mid-Atlantic Ridge? | A line where plates pull apart and new sea floor forms | A mountain range made by two continents crashing together | A trench where one plate dives under another | A row of coral reefs across the ocean
exercise: What happens in a subduction zone? | The denser plate dives under the other, making a trench and volcanoes | Two plates slide sideways past each other, and nothing new is made | Two continents push up a high mountain range | New crust rises up between two plates
exercise: Why do huge mountains rise where two continents collide? | Continental crust is too light to sink, so it crumples upwards | The mantle is squeezed out onto the surface and piles up in great heaps | Volcanoes pile up lava into peaks | The sea floor dries out and rises
exercise: What happens where plates slide past each other sideways? | Crust is neither made nor destroyed, but strong earthquakes happen | New crust is made and volcanoes erupt all along the line, as in Iceland | One plate sinks into the mantle | High mountains slowly grow there
exercise: What is the Ring of Fire? | The belt around the Pacific with most active volcanoes and big earthquakes | A ring of volcanoes around the North Pole | The hot boundary between the mantle and the core, where the iron glows red | A chain of deserts around the equator
exercise: Roughly what share of the world's active volcanoes lie around the Pacific? | About three-quarters | About a tenth of them | About a quarter of them | All of them, every one
exercise: Who proposed in 1912 that the continents drift? | Alfred Wegener | Sir Isaac Newton | Charles Darwin, the naturalist | Christopher Columbus
exercise: Why did most scientists reject Wegener's idea for decades? | He could not explain what force moved the continents | He had no maps of the continents | He said the Earth was flat | He had drawn the coasts the wrong shape so they could fit
exercise: What do the magnetic stripes on the sea floor show? | New crust forms at ridges and spreads out both ways | The sea floor is made of iron that rose up from the core | Ships have dropped magnets over the centuries | The oceans were once frozen solid
exercise: How do scientists measure plate movement today? | With GPS stations tracked by satellites | By watching the stars move across the sky each night | By measuring the length of rivers | By timing the tides every month
exercise: How fast are Europe and North America moving apart? | About 2.5 cm a year | About 2.5 metres a year | About 2.5 km a year | They are moving closer together
test: Leo says the plates float on a sea of liquid lava. Put him right. | They ride on solid mantle rock that flows very slowly | He's right, the mantle is runny lava | They float on the liquid inner core, which is made of hot iron | They sit on a layer of seawater
test: "my nails grow like 3 cm a year, so plates are basically the same speed??" Is that roughly right? | Yes, plates move about 2-10 cm a year | No, plates move about 3 km a year, mate | No, plates move faster than a car on a road | No, plates don't move at all, they're stuck
test: What would happen if no plate ever sank into the mantle any more? | The main force pushing plates, slab pull, would stop | Plates would move much faster | New mountains would grow everywhere, on every continent, very fast | The Earth would grow bigger each year
test: The Himalayas still grow a few millimetres a year. Which kind of plate edge explains this? | Two continents colliding and crumpling the crust upwards | Two plates pulling apart and making new sea floor | An ocean plate sinking under a continent | Two plates sliding past sideways
test: Japan has deep trenches offshore and a line of volcanoes. What is happening under it? | An ocean plate is diving down in a subduction zone | Two continents are colliding there | The plates are pulling apart there | Nothing, Japan sits safely in the middle of a big plate
test: "san andreas fault, mate, does it make volcanoes or what?" | No, plates slide sideways there, so it gives earthquakes, not volcanoes | Yes, it is the biggest volcano belt in the world, bigger than the whole of Japan | Yes, magma rises where the plates pull apart | No, nothing at all happens there
test: In a film, a crack opens and California floats off into the Pacific overnight. What's wrong? | Plates slide past each other a few cm a year, not open in a night | California is not on any plate | Plates move only in winter | Nothing, that really happens every century or so, after the big earthquakes
test: Iceland sits right on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge. What would you expect there? | Volcanoes and the land slowly being pulled apart | A deep ocean trench | Huge mountains made by colliding continents | No earthquakes or volcanoes at all
test: Why did Wegener's idea win in the end? | Magnetic stripes on the sea floor showed new crust spreading from the ridges | Wegener found a map from Roman times that showed the continents joined | Astronauts saw the continents moving | The idea was voted for by schoolchildren
test: Your mate says the Atlantic is shrinking, so New York is getting closer to London. Right? | No, GPS shows them moving apart about 2.5 cm a year | Yes, about 2.5 cm closer each year | Yes, about a metre closer each year | No, the distance has never changed at all, not by a single centimetre
exam: Identify the principal force to which the motion of the lithospheric plates is attributed. | The descent of cold, dense oceanic lithosphere into the mantle, termed slab pull | The gravitational attraction exerted upon the continents by the Moon and the Sun together | The pressure of prevailing winds upon the continental surfaces | The flow of rivers across the margins of the continents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a divergent plate boundary such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge? | Plates separate and ascending magma forms new crust | One plate descends beneath another into a trench | Two continental masses collide and form mountains | Plates move laterally and crust is neither formed nor destroyed
exam: It is given that two continental plates converge. Ascertain the consequence that is consistent with the established facts. | Neither plate is subducted and the crust is uplifted into a mountain range | The lighter plate descends into the mantle forming a trench | A mid-ocean ridge is formed between the two plates | Both plates melt entirely and are replaced by oceanic crust
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the reception of Wegener's hypothesis of continental drift. | It was proposed in 1912 and largely rejected until the evidence of the 1960s | It was universally accepted upon its publication in 1912 | It was proposed in the 1960s and accepted immediately by every geologist | It was refuted conclusively and has since been abandoned
